      "obligation": "refuter:11:1",
      "id": "def-partial-function-computed-by-a-machine",
      "location": "Definition",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The definition quantifies over an arbitrary deterministic one-tape machine M, whose input alphabet is an arbitrary Sigma, but then requires computation on e_X(x) in {0,1}*. The computation definition fixes its input as w in Sigma*. No condition {0,1} subseteq Sigma (or Sigma={0,1}) is stated. For example, with Sigma={a} and e_X(x)=0, the phrase “the computation of M on input 0” is outside the defined machine-input domain.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "e48d06d18f956f61b0ae5ec19774378e4049a4d7b8b845a096f188d60488aebf"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:11:2",
      "id": "prop-valid-computation-histories-are-decidable",
      "location": "Proof steps 1.1 and 2.1",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 says a valid configuration word can be recognized by having “exactly one state symbol and otherwise only tape symbols.” But the machine definition requires no disjointness of Q and Gamma. Take Q={q_0,q_acc,q_rej}, Gamma={q_0,square}, Sigma={q_0}, and a total delta with delta(q_0,q_0)=(q_acc,q_0,R). This is permitted. On input q_0, the initial configuration word prescribed by def-turing-machine-configuration is q_0q_0; both occurrences are state symbols because q_0 is in Q, so the proposed test rejects a genuine configuration word. Tags or Q cap Gamma=empty are needed.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "f2a9aaee048d874a0da6ad5db236cdd504ca2a7ad52c12da17978186dcc044c5"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:11:3",
      "id": "prop-valid-computation-histories-are-decidable",
      "location": "Proof step 4.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 4.1 claims that the step-3.1 test passes exactly for encoded accepting histories. Yet step 2.1 accepts a configuration-word candidate with an extra final blank: it only tests one state symbol and tape symbols. The configuration-word definition fixes r=max({h} union supp(t)), so appending a blank after position r is not a configuration word and is not in the step-1.1 configuration encoding image. It nevertheless parses to the same configuration, so a history code containing that redundant-blank candidate passes all of step 3.1 whenever the corresponding canonical accepting history does. Thus the described machine accepts codes outside H_{M,w}.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "f2a9aaee048d874a0da6ad5db236cdd504ca2a7ad52c12da17978186dcc044c5"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:11:4",
      "id": "lem-acceptance-is-configuration-reachability",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions [L4] and proof steps 1.2–2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "[L4] invokes def-directed-walk-trail-path-cycle-and-strong-connectivity, whose definition begins “Let D=(V,A) be a finite digraph.” In contrast, def-configuration-graph explicitly says the configuration graph “may be infinite.” For a machine that remains in q_0 and moves right on every blank, G_M(epsilon) has a distinct configuration for every head position and is infinite. The cited finite-digraph definition therefore does not license applying its directed-path terminology to this G_M(epsilon) without an extension to arbitrary digraphs.",
